      \field{abstract}{This paper presents a control system that unifies the whole-body control (WBC) and model predictive control (MPC) for quadrupedal robots. Modern robotic systems are described by high dimensional dynamics to properly capture the behavior of the system. This complicates control design; while a more precise model more accurately captures the dynamic behavior, it may not be conducive to fast control calculations. It thus becomes crucial to properly balance model complexity and control speed to control the system. We propose a control strategy which effectively distributes the model complexity between the WBC and MPC. This distribution utilizes the strength of each controller, complex-model MPC for locomotion planning and simple-model WBC for state regulation and force control, in a synergistic manner. This decomposition allows the algorithms to properly account for other subtleties that are not well captured in the simple model, such as the joint limits. This decomposition method allows the algorithm to run on-line in real-time on a modern quadrupedal robot system. The performance of this approach is verified on the Mini-Cheetah quadruped robot, demonstrating dynamic locomotion over a variety of terrain and outperforming other state of the art control methods.}
